item.text:
Exposure looks like `limited_exposure_with_choices`: Mexico had real diplomatic and civilian-nuclear-safety choices in 2022, but no nuclear-weapon command authority.

**Reused evidence**
- None. No prior accepted web evidence was available for this chapter.

**New evidence**
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Discurso del presidente Andrés Manuel López Obrador durante el desfile cívico militar: 212 Aniversario de la Independencia","publisher":"Presidencia de México","publication_date":"2022-09-16","url":"https://amlo.presidente.gob.mx/discurso-del-presidente-andres-manuel-lopez-obrador-durante-el-desfile-civico-militar-212-aniversario-de-la-independencia/","claim":"AMLO announced a five-year world truce proposal that would suspend military actions, nuclear tests, and missile tests and create a mediation committee for Ukraine.","locator":"paragraphs 28-35","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official presidential transcript with date and first-person ruler statement.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 ruler-period statement with precise date.","ruler_attribution":"Direct AMLO statement and proposal; the foreign ministry was assigned to present it at the UN.","contrary_evidence":["It is a proposal rather than an implemented policy or treaty.","It does not show command over nuclear forces because Mexico has none."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.2","1B.4","1B.8"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Common Sense, Cooperation Must Guide Way Forward towards Ensuring Safety of Zaporizhzhia Nuclear Power Plant, Secretary-General Tells Security Council","publisher":"United Nations","publication_date":"2022-09-06","url":"https://press.un.org/en/2022/sgsm21436.doc.htm","claim":"Mexico called for a security perimeter around Zaporizhzhia and a demilitarized zone so IAEA inspections could proceed safely.","locator":"Mexico statement in the Security Council briefing on Zaporizhzhia","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UN Security Council meeting coverage with attributed national intervention.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 target year and tied to the Zaporizhzhia crisis.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect state attribution through Mexico's UN mission; relevant to AMLO only as foreign-policy posture, not personal nuclear command.","contrary_evidence":["This addresses a foreign crisis, not Mexico's own nuclear command-and-control.","It is diplomatic advocacy, not an operational safety action inside Mexico."],"lenses":["1B.2","1B.8"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Fighting at Ukraine Nuclear Power Site Irresponsible, against Geneva Conventions, Under-Secretary-General Tells Security Council","publisher":"United Nations","publication_date":"2022-09-06","url":"https://press.un.org/en/2022/sc15118.doc.htm","claim":"Mexico warned that a nuclear accident at Zaporizhzhia would have unacceptable humanitarian and environmental consequences and called for a security perimeter around the plant.","locator":"Mexico intervention in the Security Council emergency meeting on attacks","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UN meeting coverage with an attributed Mexico intervention on nuclear-site risk.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 crisis-period evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect state attribution through Mexico's UN mission; useful as a ruler-period foreign-policy signal rather than direct nuclear command.","contrary_evidence":["The evidence concerns a crisis abroad, not Mexican nuclear infrastructure.","It does not demonstrate internal command-and-control reform."],"lenses":["1B.2","1B.8","1B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"‘False Logic’ of Deterrence Elevates Role of Nuclear Weapons in Security Doctrines, First Committee Hears as General Debate Continues","publisher":"United Nations","publication_date":"2022-10-06","url":"https://press.un.org/en/2022/gadis3684.doc.htm","claim":"Mexico rejected the false logic of deterrence and stressed the need to avoid the catastrophic humanitarian consequences of a nuclear detonation.","locator":"First Committee general debate, Mexico intervention","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UN First Committee coverage with an attributed Mexico statement on nuclear doctrine.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Strong 2022 period fit during the disarmament season.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect state attribution via Mexico's diplomatic mission; reflects the AMLO government's foreign-policy line.","contrary_evidence":["This is rhetorical and multilateral, not an operational nuclear policy change.","It does not prove personal hands-on management by AMLO."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.2","1B.4"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Non-Nuclear-Armed States Get Nothing in Return for Fulfilling Commitments Except Threat of Potential Annihilation, First Committee Told","publisher":"United Nations","publication_date":"2022-10-14","url":"https://press.un.org/en/2022/gadis3690.doc.htm","claim":"Mexico said non-nuclear-weapon States fulfill disarmament commitments without getting anything in return except a vision of potential annihilation, and that the claim nuclear weapons guarantee security is unsustainable and immoral.","locator":"First Committee thematic segment on nuclear weapons","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UN official meeting record summarizing an attributed Mexico intervention.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022 and squarely on nuclear-risk responsibility.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect state attribution through Mexico's permanent mission; relevant to AMLO as the government's external posture.","contrary_evidence":["This is normative advocacy, not proof of domestic safety governance.","It does not by itself show concrete risk reduction at home."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.2","1B.4"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Conclusion of the first meeting of the Nuclear Weapons Ban Treaty in Vienna","publisher":"Secretaría de Relaciones Exteriores","publication_date":"2022-06-24","url":"https://www.gob.mx/sre/prensa/conclusion-of-the-first-meeting-of-the-nuclear-weapons-ban-treaty-in-vienna","claim":"Mexico welcomed the first meeting of States Parties to the TPNW and described itself as one of the treaty's architects, linking that role to the Treaty of Tlatelolco tradition.","locator":"press release summary and lead paragraphs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official foreign-ministry release; the page itself was blocked, but the search snippet captured the core claim clearly.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Good 2022 period fit for arms-control posture.","ruler_attribution":"State-level foreign-policy attribution; not a personal command action by AMLO, but it does reflect the administration's line.","contrary_evidence":["It is a celebratory treaty statement, not evidence of enforcement power.","This is supportive of disarmament norms rather than proof of broader existential-risk governance."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.4","1B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Treaty on the Prohibition of Nuclear Weapons","publisher":"United Nations Treaty Collection","publication_date":"2021-01-22","url":"https://treaties.un.org/Pages/showActionDetails.aspx?clang=_en&objid=08000002804e4e95","claim":"Mexico is listed as having ratified the Treaty on the Prohibition of Nuclear Weapons, with effect from 22 January 2021.","locator":"participant action table for Mexico","source_type":"legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Treaty-collection action record with participant, action, and effect date.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Establishes the inherited treaty baseline in force during 2022.","ruler_attribution":"State baseline rather than personal attribution; relevant because it constrains and signals Mexico's non-nuclear policy posture.","contrary_evidence":["Ratification does not itself prove 2022 implementation quality.","It is a structural commitment, not a ruler-discretion choice made during 2022."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.6","1B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Treaty on the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons","publisher":"United Nations Treaty Collection","publication_date":"1970-05-25","url":"https://treaties.un.org/Pages/showActionDetails.aspx?clang=_en&objid=0800000280120b8d","claim":"Mexico ratified the NPT on 25 May 1970.","locator":"Mexico participant action record","source_type":"legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Treaty-collection action record with participant, action, and registration date.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Long-run baseline that still governs the 2022 period.","ruler_attribution":"State baseline rather than personal attribution; it frames Mexico as a non-nuclear-weapon state party.","contrary_evidence":["Historical ratification does not show how actively the 2022 government enforced the treaty.","It is a baseline constraint, not a ruler-level performance indicator."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.4","1B.6","1B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Treaty on the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons","publisher":"United Nations","publication_date":"2015-04-27","url":"https://www.un.org/en/conf/npt/2015/text.shtml","claim":"The NPT text requires non-nuclear-weapon States not to manufacture or acquire nuclear weapons and to accept IAEA safeguards, and it defines nuclear-weapon States as those that had manufactured and exploded a nuclear weapon or other nuclear explosive device before 1 January 1967.","locator":"Articles II, III, and IX","source_type":"legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary treaty text from the United Nations; exact obligations are explicit.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Permanent legal framework governing Mexico in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Contextual baseline only; Mexico's status under the treaty constrains the ruler's nuclear exposure.","contrary_evidence":["This is treaty law, not evidence of ruler behavior in 2022.","It supports exposure assessment more than performance judgment."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.4","1B.6","1B.9"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Treaty for the Prohibition of Nuclear Weapons in Latin America","publisher":"United Nations Treaty Collection","publication_date":"1967-02-14","url":"https://treaties.un.org/Pages/showDetails.aspx?objid=08000002801273c1","claim":"The Treaty of Tlatelolco was concluded in Mexico City, Mexico is the depositary, and the treaty establishes a Latin American nuclear-weapon-free zone.","locator":"title record and participant/depositary fields","source_type":"legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary treaty registration record with participant and depositary metadata.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Historical baseline that remained fully relevant in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"State-level institutional baseline, not a personal AMLO action.","contrary_evidence":["This is a standing regional regime, not a 2022 ruler choice.","It limits nuclear exposure but does not prove active risk reduction."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.6","1B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico (United Mexican States)","publisher":"IAEA Office of Legal Affairs","publication_date":"2026-02-24","url":"https://ola.iaea.org/Applications/FactSheets/Country/Detail?code=MX","claim":"Mexico has a safeguards agreement in force under the NPT and Tlatelolco framework and an Additional Protocol in force with the IAEA.","locator":"Safeguards Agreements section","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official IAEA country fact sheet listing instruments and in-force status.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Structural safeguard baseline that was already in place during 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Institutional and legal context, not direct AMLO conduct.","contrary_evidence":["Safeguards in force do not imply personal nuclear leadership by the ruler.","The record is about compliance architecture, not crisis behavior."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.4","1B.9","1B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"PRIS - Country Details","publisher":"IAEA PRIS","publication_date":"2026-07-08","url":"https://pris.iaea.org/pris/CountryStatistics/CountryDetails.aspx?current=MX","claim":"Mexico had two nuclear power reactors in operation, and the database shows a 2022 nuclear share of 4.5 percent.","locator":"summary table and nuclear share trend","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official reactor database with country summary and time series.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"The 2022 data point directly fits the target year; the reactor count is stable context.","ruler_attribution":"Country-level civilian nuclear context rather than personal ruler command.","contrary_evidence":["Civilian reactors are not nuclear weapons.","This is exposure context, not proof of ruler-level risk management."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.9","1B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IAEA Concludes Long Term Operational Safety Review at Mexico’s Laguna Verde Nuclear Power Plant","publisher":"IAEA","publication_date":"2022-06-27","url":"https://nucleus-qa.iaea.org/sites/connect/_layouts/15/listform.aspx?ContentTypeID=0x010098D54B64E39A354DB05F7672AB6C615E&ID=221&ListId=%7B76F64203-9B4B-4C8A-862E-8B393FEE1E6D%7D&PageType=4","claim":"An IAEA team completed a review of long-term operational safety at the Laguna Verde Nuclear Power Plant in 2022.","locator":"news description and publication metadata","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"IAEA news metadata is official and dated, though the page is an index entry rather than a full article.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Excellent 2022 period fit for civilian nuclear safety context.","ruler_attribution":"Institutional safety context; at most indirectly attributable to the AMLO government through regulatory stewardship.","contrary_evidence":["A peer review is not a verdict on the government's overall safety record.","It does not show personal presidential control over the plant."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.9"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Gobierno de México y gobierno de Veracruz anuncian simulacro en las inmediaciones de la Central Nucleoeléctrica Laguna Verde","publisher":"Gobierno de México, SSPC/CNPC/CFE","publication_date":"2022-08-22","url":"https://seguridad.sspc.gob.mx/contenido/1358/acciones/6/acciones/1/onprenna","claim":"Mexico announced the 2022 integrated external radiological emergency exercise near Laguna Verde as a preventive civil-protection measure in line with nuclear and radiological safety requirements.","locator":"press release body describing PERE 2022","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official government release with a date and specific exercise description; the page itself was not fully retrievable here.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 period fit for emergency-preparedness context.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect institutional attribution; it reflects the Mexican state apparatus under AMLO rather than a personal nuclear decision.","contrary_evidence":["A drill is not evidence of a crisis or accident.","It is preparedness context, not direct proof of improved governance."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.9"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Conclusion of the first meeting of the Nuclear Weapons Ban Treaty in Vienna","publisher":"Secretaría de Relaciones Exteriores","publication_date":"2022-06-24","url":"https://www.gob.mx/sre/prensa/conclusion-of-the-first-meeting-of-the-nuclear-weapons-ban-treaty-in-vienna","claim":"Mexico welcomed the successful conclusion of the first TPNW meeting and framed itself as one of the treaty's architects with a tradition of nuclear disarmament leadership.","locator":"press release summary and opening paragraphs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official foreign-ministry release; the page was blocked but the indexed summary is clear and specific.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022 and relevant to nuclear-disarmament diplomacy.","ruler_attribution":"State foreign-policy posture under AMLO; indirect but useful evidence of the government's alignment.","contrary_evidence":["Treaty advocacy is not the same as operational safety governance.","It does not show any nuclear command authority because Mexico lacks nuclear weapons."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.4","1B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
- I considered roughly 30 to 35 documents across official Mexican presidency/SRE pages, UN treaty records, UN press coverage, IAEA/PRIS pages, and background reporting from Reuters, AP, WNA, NTI, FAS, and Mexican newspapers.
- I retained 14 source URLs because they were the best non-duplicative fit for 1B and were either primary, official, or strong independent monitoring.
- I rejected `12.09.22` AMLO transcript coverage as a near-duplicate of the `16.09.22` official speech.
- I rejected Reuters/AP/Euronews stories on the same AMLO peace proposal because the presidential transcript was stronger and more direct.
- I rejected generic Ukraine war and Russia nuclear-threat stories that did not mention Mexico.
- I rejected broad TPNW/NPT explainers from NTI/FAS where the treaty text or UN treaty records were already available.
- I rejected later 2023-2026 Mexican nuclear diplomacy pages when they added no materially distinct 2022 evidence.
- I rejected older historical Tlatelolco background pieces once the UN treaty record and UN NWFZ overview covered the institutional baseline.
- I rejected broad civilian-energy profiles that duplicated PRIS or IAEA safety material without adding ruler-period specificity.

**Remaining gaps**
- There is still no direct evidence that AMLO personally managed nuclear-safety appointments or crisis protocols in 2022. That is probably a structural limitation, not a search failure.
- I did not find any credible 2022 AMLO-linked decision in cyber, biological, or AI catastrophic-risk policy that rises to chapter-1B relevance.
- If you want deeper 1B.3 coverage, the next best search would be Mexico CNSNS/SENER appointment and regulatory records for 2022, but the yield is likely low.
- If you want tighter ruler attribution, a next pass could look for presidential or cabinet-level orders on Laguna Verde or disarmament diplomacy, but the current record already supports a careful no-score dossier.
